WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.620
Press a Pacinian corpuscle and hold.

2
00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:12.060
The nerve fibre fires when the press starts, and again when it ends.

3
00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:14.700
It does not fire during the hold.

4
00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:21.520
A Pacinian corpuscle wraps one nerve ending in many concentric layers, like an

5
00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:25.140
onion. For decades, those layers got the credit.

6
00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:26.940
Was that right?

7
00:00:27.283 --> 00:00:30.143
The classic idea was a mechanical filter.

8
00:00:30.883 --> 00:00:35.423
In 1966, Loewenstein and Skalak modelled the capsule.

9
00:00:36.183 --> 00:00:40.223
In their model, fluid between the layers supplies viscosity.

10
00:00:40.943 --> 00:00:42.803
The layers supply elasticity.

11
00:00:43.483 --> 00:00:47.023
A slow press is dissipated before it reaches the core.

12
00:00:47.663 --> 00:00:49.203
A fast tap gets through.

13
00:00:50.083 --> 00:00:53.963
For decades, this high-pass filter was the textbook explanation.

14
00:00:54.967 --> 00:01:02.207
A year earlier, in 1965, Loewenstein and Mendelson had removed most of the layers

15
00:01:02.207 --> 00:01:04.227
from isolated cat corpuscles.

16
00:01:04.987 --> 00:01:06.387
Then they held a press.

17
00:01:07.367 --> 00:01:12.547
After that, the generator potential, the graded depolarization of the nerve ending,

18
00:01:13.027 --> 00:01:14.807
decayed much more slowly.

19
00:01:15.947 --> 00:01:19.987
Later reviewers note this appeared mainly under intense compression.

20
00:01:20.747 --> 00:01:23.647
The ending typically still fired a single spike.

21
00:01:24.357 --> 00:01:26.747
So adaptation had two stages.

22
00:01:27.507 --> 00:01:30.127
One lay in the mechanics around the ending.

23
00:01:30.627 --> 00:01:33.167
The other lay in its spike-generating membrane.

24
00:01:34.167 --> 00:01:38.047
In 2025, the test moved to duck bill skin.

25
00:01:38.987 --> 00:01:43.927
Ziolkowski and colleagues recorded directly from the nerve terminal inside Herbst

26
00:01:43.927 --> 00:01:46.807
corpuscles, the bird version of Pacinian ones.

27
00:01:47.367 --> 00:01:51.387
The skin came from late embryos, and the recordings were ex vivo.

28
00:01:52.187 --> 00:01:56.527
They removed the outer core and left the inner core's lamellar cells in place.

29
00:01:57.267 --> 00:01:59.447
The fast transduction current stayed.

30
00:02:00.067 --> 00:02:02.287
The frequency tuning did not change significantly.

31
00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:08.920
Slower inactivation appeared only as decapsulated terminals deteriorated.

32
00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:13.620
So a dying terminal can look like one that lost its filter.

33
00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:16.420
That is one reading of 1965.

34
00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:21.180
Keep the scope. This is a bird corpuscle, ex vivo.

35
00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:26.540
It does not show that the capsule does nothing in a cat or a human hand.

36
00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:29.990
So what are the lamellar cells for?

37
00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:33.730
These flattened cells wrap the nerve terminal.

38
00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:37.370
In duck bill skin, they respond to force too.

39
00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:42.670
Depolarizing one Grandry lamellar cell could make its afferent fire.

40
00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:48.530
But in duck, the delay ran from 20 milliseconds to about 9 seconds.

41
00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:55.370
A 200 Hz vibration repeats every 5 milliseconds, so lamellar cells may set the

42
00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:58.390
terminal's sensitivity, not carry each cycle.

43
00:02:59.630 --> 00:03:05.550
Keep this. In duck corpuscles, with the outer core gone, the terminal still adapts fast.
